# THE OPERATIONS THAT BOTH DRAW AND INDUCE -- the entries DᵀN would have. One row per
# (operation, drawn-from layer, committed-to layer): an operation consuming from one layer
# while committing on another is a cross-layer edge, and the join on the shared operation
# index is what a matrix product IS.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE INCIDENCE COMPOSES; THE QUANTITIES DO NOT. Multiply the two magnitudes and the
#    answer comes out in people·launches, which is not a rate of anything, because each
#    layer carries its own unit. So this relation reports REACHABILITY and leaves the
#    numbers beside it unmultiplied. There is also no firing count per operation, on
#    purpose: sequence and timing are BPMN's job and this model does not compete for it.
#
# ⭐ IN THIS CORPUS EXACTLY ONE OPERATION QUALIFIES AND ITS DRAW IS UNMEASURED, so the
#   product cannot be computed at all. The matrix that would show cross-layer structure is
#   empty precisely because the interesting quantity has no instrument behind it, which is
#   the thing this model exists to be able to say.
-- the shared index is (filing, operation); pm:Operation carries both children.
SELECT d.filing, d.operation,
       d.layer AS drawn_from, d.mode AS drawn,
       n.layer AS commits,    n.mode AS committed,
       coalesce(d.unit, '(unmeasured)') || ' * '
    || coalesce(n.unit, '(unmeasured)') AS the_unit_that_warns_you,
       d.mode * n.mode                    AS the_product_nobody_should_use
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/draws.sqlc)
) d
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc)
) n USING (filing, operation)
